11. Matcher Methods (🔥 VERY IMPORTANT)
| Method      | Purpose          |
| ----------- | ---------------- |
| `find()`    | finds next match |
| `matches()` | full match       |
| `group()`   | matched value    |
| `start()`   | start index      |
| `end()`     | end index        |


Example
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\d+");
Matcher m = p.matcher("Order 123 and 456");

while (m.find()) {
    System.out.println(m.group());
}
Output
123
456

12. matches() vs find() (🔥 Tricky)
"123abc".matches("\\d+"); // false
✔ matches() → full string match
✔ find() → partial match

13. String Class RegEx Methods
Java String has built-in RegEx support.

13.1 matches()
System.out.println("abc123".matches("[a-z]+\\d+"));
Output
true

13.2 replaceAll()
System.out.println("a1b2c3".replaceAll("\\d", ""));
Output
abc

13.3 split()
String[] arr = "Java,Spring,Boot".split(",");
for (String s : arr) {
    System.out.println(s);
}
Output
Java
Spring
Boot

14. Common Validation Examples (🔥 MUST PREPARE)
14.1 Email Validation
String email = "test@gmail.com";
System.out.println(email.matches("^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@(.+)$"));

Output
true

14.2 Mobile Number (India)
System.out.println("9876543210".matches("[6-9][0-9]{9}"));

Output
true

14.3 Password Validation
At least 1 digit, 1 uppercase, 8 chars:
System.out.println("Abc@1234".matches("(?=.*\\d)(?=.*[A-Z]).{8,}"));

Output
true

20. Common Interview Questions (4+ Years)

Q1. matches() vs find()?
matches → full match
find → partial

Q2. Why double slash in Java RegEx?
Escape sequence for string literal

Q3. Best way to validate input?
Anchored regex (^ and $)

Q4. Is RegEx thread-safe?
Pattern → yes
Matcher → no
